@node Top
@top ny - Nytrix Tool

@command{ny} runs source, emits native binaries, opens the REPL, and dispatches
the built-in project tools.

Feature support varies across LLVM, the Nytrix-owned native backend, and
WebAssembly. See @file{docs/learn/status.md} and
@file{docs/learn/quirks.md} before relying on a backend or partial
contract.

@menu
* Run::          Source execution and native output.
* Subcommands::  Project, package, docs, format, test, and perf tools.
* Options::      Common flags.
* Environment::  Common environment variables.
* Examples::     Short command examples.
* See Also::     Markdown documentation.
@end menu

@node Run
@chapter Run

@table @code
@item ny
Start the REPL, or read piped stdin as REPL batch input.
@item ny file.ny
Compile and run a file through the default JIT path.
@item ny -c @var{CODE}
Run inline source.
@item ny -ic @var{CODE}, ny -ci @var{CODE}
Run inline source, then continue in the REPL.
@item ny --repl < file.ny
Compile stdin source once through the REPL batch path.
@item ny --run=aot file.ny
Build a temporary native executable and run it.
@item ny --output=app file.ny
Emit a native executable.
@item ny --repl, ny --plain-repl
Start an explicit REPL. @code{--plain-repl} disables line-editing UI.
@end table

Prompt commands include @code{:snapshot [snapfile] [-o executable]} and
@code{:load snapfile}.

@node Subcommands
@chapter Subcommands

@table @code
@item ny new [dir]
Create a project scaffold with manifest, source entry, README, and gitignore.
@item ny pkg @var{command}
Manage source packages, repositories, lockfiles, roots, and installs.
@item ny fmt [mode] [paths...]
Format Nytrix source and audit for trim, bloat, bugs, modules, profiles,
layouts, contracts, FFI, constants, specialization, and compile-time
generation candidates.
@item ny doc search [--docs|--symbols] query
Search prose pages, modules, exported symbols, docstrings, and keyword tags.
@item ny doc get query
Print the closest reference entry.
@item ny doc -o docs
Generate the static HTML reference.
@item ny test [paths...] [--pattern name]
Run runtime, error, stdlib, or selected test checks.
@item ny perf
Run maintained performance checks and comparison workflows.
@item ny make [target]
Native build wrapper. Common targets: @code{bin}, @code{test}, @code{std},
@code{docs}, @code{perf}, @code{install}.
@end table

@section Package Commands

@table @code
@item ny pkg init [name]
Create @file{ny.pkg.json}.
@item ny pkg add name source
Add, install, and lock a dependency.
@item ny get name
Resolve a registry or repository package and install it.
@item ny pkg install, sync, update, uninstall, list
Maintain installed dependencies and the lockfile.
@item ny pkg repo add/list/sync/path/remove
Manage package repositories.
@item ny pkg search [--interactive] query
Search packages. The interactive picker is built into @command{ny}.
@item ny pkg path, info, venv, registry
Inspect package roots, metadata, venv path, and registries.
@end table

Source forms include local paths, @code{git+} URLs, SSH git sources, archives,
and repository packages. Install roots include @file{./ny_modules},
@file{./vendor/ny_modules}, @file{./.nytrix/venv/lib}, user/global roots,
system roots, and @code{--root path}.

There is no default package repository or implicit public-registry fallback.
Add repositories you trust with @code{ny pkg repo add}, or pass an explicit
source. Search discovers packages from configured repositories and registry
entries; it does not certify their safety. This is source dependency management,
not a dependency-injection framework. Content hashes identify bytes, not a
trusted publisher. Inspect active configuration with @code{ny pkg registry} and
@code{ny pkg repo list}.

@node Options
@chapter Options

The complete flag reference is generated from the compiler's option registry:
run @code{ny --help all}. Use @code{ny --help @var{topic}} or
@code{ny --help short} for focused help and short-option families.

@section Invocation And Inspection

@table @code
@item --run=auto|aot|jit|native|none
Select execution. @code{aot} builds and runs a native executable;
@code{none} compiles without executing.
@item --mode=safe|fast|bare
Select runtime safety and compatibility policy.
@item --timeout=SECONDS
Limit program runtime. The legacy @code{-timeout SECONDS} spelling remains
accepted and resolves to the same option.
@item --target=HOST|wasm|TRIPLE
Choose a target preset or host triple.
@item -c CODE
Execute inline source. Put program arguments after @code{--}.
@item @@FILE
Read compiler arguments from a quoted response file. Nested files are
supported; @code{--} stops option expansion and preserves following program
arguments.
@item --help [TOPIC], --help all, --help=json
Show concise help, a focused option topic, the full option list, or JSON
registry metadata.
@item --presets, +debug, +release, +ci, +inspect, +perf
List or select a built-in option preset. Later command-line options can
override preset values.
@item --completions=bash|zsh|fish
Print shell completion definitions.
@item -n, --print-flags[=json]
Print recognized compiler options in canonical form and show the program
argument boundary. Output includes provenance for defaults (implicit, with no
explicit default flags) and the user, project, environment, and command-line
sources. JSON mode emits the flags, provenance, and program arguments as JSON.
@end table

@section Configuration Files

Configuration precedence is defaults, user config, project config,
@env{NYTRIX_FLAGS} (or the compatibility fallback @env{NY_FLAGS}), then
command-line options. Later values override earlier values according to
each option's semantics.

The user file is
@file{~/.config/nytrix/ny.toml} (or the corresponding
@file{%USERPROFILE%/.config/nytrix/ny.toml} on Windows); the project file is
@file{./ny.toml}. Files use Nytrix's documented TOML subset, not arbitrary
TOML: @code{[options]} or @code{[flags]} sections contain option names without
leading dashes and scalar values, while only the user file may contain an
@code{[alias]} section. A boolean option uses @code{true} or @code{false};
list options accept quoted comma-separated values or arrays.

@example
[options]
run = "none"
opt-level = 2

[alias]
dbg = "-Dnv -Ea -vv"
@end example

Use @code{ny +dbg file.ny} to expand a user alias. Built-in presets
(@code{+debug}, @code{+release}, @code{+ci}, @code{+inspect}, and @code{+perf})
take precedence and cannot be shadowed. Environment flag strings are quoted
argument lists; they cannot contain positional program arguments or @code{--}.

Project config cannot set registry-marked code-execution or path-injection
options. Those values are ignored with a warning unless
@code{--trust-config} appears on the command line before the program input.
User config and command-line values remain trusted.

@section Diagnostics And Safety

@table @code
@item --color=auto|always|never
Control diagnostic color.
@item --diag-compact
Print compact diagnostics.
@item --collect-errors
Continue compilation to collect diagnostics.
@item --diag-rich
Print wider source context.
@item --mode=safe
Enable the strict runtime safety policy.
@item --strict
Enable strict check presets.
@item --check=LIST
Select checks such as @code{types}, @code{effects}, @code{alias},
@code{alias-escape}, @code{ownership}, or @code{shadow}; prefix a supported
check with @code{no-} to disable it.
@item --borrow-check
Enable advisory borrow and ownership diagnostics.
@item --ownership-check=off|advisory|strict
Choose the ownership diagnostic level.
@item --heap=manual|raii|rc|gc
Choose heap ownership policy. Manual ownership is the default; tracing
collection is optional and selected only with @code{gc}.
@item --warn=none|useful|all|error
Set the compiler warning policy.
@end table

@section Optimization And Output

@table @code
@item --opt-level=0|1|2|3|s|z
Set optimization level. Use an explicit value; a bare @code{-O} is an error.
@item --opt-profile=speed|balanced|compile|size|none|peak
Select the optimization profile. @code{peak} favors native benchmark speed over
compile latency.
@item --opt=LIST
Select optimizer features such as @code{dce}, @code{loops},
@code{internalize}, @code{autotune}, @code{lto}, and @code{fast-math}.
@item --fast
High optimization convenience mode with stripped output by default.
@item --release
Select the size-oriented AOT release preset.
@item --run=none
Compile without executing.
@item --output=PATH
Write the compiled executable or selected output to a path.
@item --strip, --no-strip
Choose stripped or unstripped native output.
@item -L DIR, -l LIB
Add native linker search paths and libraries.
@item --emit=KIND[=PATH],...
Select outputs: @code{asm}, @code{ir}, @code{bc}, @code{obj},
@code{wasm}, @code{nyr}, @code{nyr-metadata}, @code{report},
@code{artifact}, @code{refinement-interface}, @code{effect-interface},
@code{shapes}, or @code{native-bundle}. Omitted paths are derived from the
input stem where supported. Wasm output requires a Wasm-compatible target.
Legacy output switches such as @code{--emit-ir} remain accepted and map to
their corresponding @code{--emit} kind.
@end table

@section Backend And Artifact Tuning

@table @code
@item --gpu-opt=LIST
Set GPU offload mode and workload threshold, asynchronous dispatch,
floating-point policy, target, and object format.
@item --jit-opt=LIST
Set JIT engine, optimization level, code model, fast instruction selection, and
performance-map output.
@item --native-tuning=LIST
Set native tiering budgets and thresholds or prefer VM/native execution.
@item --dwarf=LIST
Set DWARF version and local, split-inlining, and profile-info records.
@item --nyr-limit=LIST
Set NYR optimizer instruction/growth limits and VM step/recursion limits.
@item --nyr-check=LIST
Select NYR verification and native translation-validation checks.
@item --nyr-pass=disable:NAME,stop-after:NAME
Disable a named NYR pass or stop immediately after it.
@item --use=LIST
Load validated artifacts, refinement/effect interfaces, or a native bundle.
@end table

@section Debugging

@table @code
@item -v, -vv, -vvv, --debug
Increase logging.
@item -time, --prof=timing|stats|gprof|compile|peak-rss
Print phase timing or select compiler/runtime profiling outputs. The legacy
@code{-prof} enables the default profiling mode, and @code{-prof=LIST} remains
accepted as an alias for @code{--prof=LIST}.
@item --dump=LIST
Select AST, tokens, LLVM, NYR, VM, function, escape, proof, ABI, stack,
register-allocation, ELF, call-graph, or expansion inspection output.
@item --trace=LIST
Select compiler trace categories such as parsing, inference, LLVM lowering,
register allocation, and execution.
@item --nyr-check=LIST
Select NYR verification, bisect, rollback, fingerprint, and native
translation-validation checks.
@item --nyr-pass=disable:NAME,stop-after:NAME
Control an individual NYR optimizer pass.
@item --dump-on-error
Write debug source/IR artifacts on failure.
@item -trace
Enable execution tracing.
@end table

@node Environment
@chapter Environment

Prefer CLI flags in shared scripts. Common environment variables:

@table @code
@item NYTRIX_CORE_PATH
Override the installed or generated @file{core.ny} bundle path.
@item NYTRIX_STD_PATH
Compatibility alias for @code{NYTRIX_CORE_PATH}.
@item NYTRIX_STD_PREBUILT, NYTRIX_BUILD_STD_PATH
Override the generated @file{core.ny} source used by internal build tools.
@item NYTRIX_HOST_TRIPLE, NYTRIX_ARM_FLOAT_ABI
Host target tuning.
@item NYTRIX_HOST_CFLAGS, NYTRIX_HOST_LDFLAGS
Host compiler and linker flags.
@item NYTRIX_FLAGS, NY_FLAGS
Compiler options inserted after project configuration and before explicit
command-line options. @code{NY_FLAGS} is used only when @code{NYTRIX_FLAGS}
is unset or empty.
@item NYTRIX_TRACE_COMPTIME_MACRO
Trace native compile-time macro expansion.
@item NYTRIX_EFFECT_ASYNC_LOWERING
Enable eligible async-effect lowering globally.
@end table

Use @code{ny --help env} for the full environment list.

@node Examples
@chapter Examples

@example
ny --version
ny -c 'print(1 + 1)'
ny file.ny
ny --run=aot file.ny
ny --opt-level=3 --opt-profile=peak --output=app bench.ny
ny fmt --check lib
ny fmt --cloc --top 20 lib
ny doc search --symbols json_decode
ny test --pattern import
ny pkg add helper ./deps/helper
@end example

